WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.116 --> 00:00:02.972
Hello and welcome back to J D Science.

2
00:00:03.251 --> 00:00:06.873
In today's G C S E Chemistry lesson, we are going

3
00:00:06.931 --> 00:00:10.194
to explore pure substances and mixtures.

4
00:00:10.472 --> 00:00:14.373
We will learn how to distinguish between them, explain

5
00:00:14.420 --> 00:00:18.472
the difference between melting and boiling behaviour, and examine

6
00:00:18.541 --> 00:00:22.640
how scientists use melting point data to assess purity.

7
00:00:22.814 --> 00:00:25.217
Let us begin with an important question.

8
00:00:25.554 --> 00:00:29.269
Is bottled mineral water a pure substance?

9
00:00:29.443 --> 00:00:33.240
You might think the answer is yes because the water looks clear

10
00:00:33.356 --> 00:00:34.540
and is safe to drink.

11
00:00:34.935 --> 00:00:38.731
However, in chemistry, the word pure has a very specific

12
00:00:38.801 --> 00:00:39.265
meaning.

13
00:00:39.544 --> 00:00:43.816
A pure substance contains only one element or one compound,

14
00:00:44.060 --> 00:00:46.754
with no other substances mixed with it.

15
00:00:46.997 --> 00:00:50.794
For example, a sample containing only copper atoms is

16
00:00:50.852 --> 00:00:51.874
a pure element.

17
00:00:52.210 --> 00:00:55.960
Similarly, a sample containing only water molecules is

18
00:00:56.030 --> 00:00:57.145
a pure compound.

19
00:00:57.760 --> 00:01:01.812
Distilled water is an example of water that can be very

20
00:01:01.905 --> 00:01:05.945
pure because most dissolved substances have been removed.

21
00:01:06.398 --> 00:01:10.600
A mixture, on the other hand, contains two or more substances

22
00:01:10.670 --> 00:01:13.004
that are not chemically bonded together.

23
00:01:13.340 --> 00:01:16.986
The substances in a mixture retain their own chemical

24
00:01:17.079 --> 00:01:21.108
identities and can often be separated using physical

25
00:01:21.189 --> 00:01:21.769
methods.

26
00:01:21.920 --> 00:01:26.158
For example, air is a mixture of gases, including nitrogen,

27
00:01:26.216 --> 00:01:28.747
oxygen, argon and carbon dioxide.

28
00:01:29.084 --> 00:01:32.787
Seawater is also a mixture because it contains water

29
00:01:32.880 --> 00:01:37.048
and dissolved salts, together with other substances.

30
00:01:37.443 --> 00:01:41.646
Now, let us consider an important difference between pure substances

31
00:01:41.727 --> 00:01:42.737
and mixtures.

32
00:01:42.911 --> 00:01:46.569
A pure substance has a sharp, characteristic melting point

33
00:01:46.685 --> 00:01:49.285
and boiling point at a given pressure.

34
00:01:49.680 --> 00:01:53.581
For example, pure water melts at zero degrees

35
00:01:53.627 --> 00:01:57.458
Celsius and boils at one hundred degrees Celsius at

36
00:01:57.575 --> 00:01:59.595
normal atmospheric pressure.

37
00:01:59.769 --> 00:02:03.530
These temperatures can be used to help identify the substance.

38
00:02:03.809 --> 00:02:07.513
Mixtures, however, often melt or boil over

39
00:02:07.594 --> 00:02:09.219
a range of temperatures.

40
00:02:09.556 --> 00:02:13.538
This is because the different substances present affect the melting

41
00:02:13.619 --> 00:02:15.198
and boiling behaviour.

42
00:02:15.593 --> 00:02:18.426
Let us consider an example involving ice.

43
00:02:18.879 --> 00:02:22.780
Pure ice melts at approximately zero degrees Celsius

44
00:02:22.989 --> 00:02:25.647
under normal atmospheric pressure.

45
00:02:26.100 --> 00:02:29.873
However, if impurities are dissolved in the water, they

46
00:02:29.908 --> 00:02:31.824
can lower its freezing point.

47
00:02:32.219 --> 00:02:35.957
This is why salt is spread on icy roads during winter.

48
00:02:36.074 --> 00:02:39.870
When salt dissolves in the water present on the ice, it

49
00:02:39.986 --> 00:02:43.794
lowers the freezing point, helping to prevent the water from

50
00:02:43.875 --> 00:02:46.465
refreezing at zero degrees Celsius.

51
00:02:46.987 --> 00:02:50.598
Now, let us consider how scientists use melting points

52
00:02:50.644 --> 00:02:51.817
to test purity.

53
00:02:52.061 --> 00:02:56.066
Suppose a scientist has a sample of a solid that is believed

54
00:02:56.113 --> 00:02:58.760
to be a particular chemical compound.

55
00:02:59.096 --> 00:03:03.067
The pure compound has a known melting point of eighty degrees

56
00:03:03.125 --> 00:03:03.938
Celsius.

57
00:03:04.275 --> 00:03:08.663
If the sample melts sharply at approximately eighty degrees Celsius,

58
00:03:08.942 --> 00:03:12.193
this supports the conclusion that it may be pure.

59
00:03:12.587 --> 00:03:16.674
However, if the sample begins melting at seventy-four

60
00:03:16.732 --> 00:03:20.412
degrees Celsius and finishes melting at seventy-nine

61
00:03:20.447 --> 00:03:24.522
degrees Celsius, this suggests that impurities may be present.

62
00:03:24.975 --> 00:03:28.969
For many solids, impurities lower the melting point and

63
00:03:29.050 --> 00:03:30.826
broaden the melting range.

64
00:03:31.349 --> 00:03:34.971
It is important to recognise that a melting point measurement

65
00:03:35.029 --> 00:03:38.930
alone does not always prove identity or complete purity,

66
00:03:39.000 --> 00:03:41.334
but it provides useful evidence.

67
00:03:41.543 --> 00:03:45.327
Now, let us compare pure water with seawater.

68
00:03:45.571 --> 00:03:49.553
Pure water contains only H two O molecules.

69
00:03:49.832 --> 00:03:53.895
Seawater contains water molecules together with dissolved salts

70
00:03:53.965 --> 00:03:55.544
and other substances.

71
00:03:55.695 --> 00:03:59.851
As a result, seawater does not have exactly the same freezing

72
00:03:59.944 --> 00:04:02.893
and boiling behaviour as pure water.

73
00:04:03.230 --> 00:04:06.852
Dissolved salts generally lower the freezing point and

74
00:04:06.968 --> 00:04:08.896
raise the boiling point of water.

75
00:04:09.070 --> 00:04:11.334
Let us try a short activity.

76
00:04:11.415 --> 00:04:13.110
I will name five materials.

77
00:04:13.354 --> 00:04:16.895
Decide whether each is a pure substance or a mixture.

78
00:04:17.232 --> 00:04:21.075
First, oxygen gas containing only O two

79
00:04:21.168 --> 00:04:22.015
molecules.

80
00:04:22.352 --> 00:04:25.940
This is a pure substance because it contains only one element.

81
00:04:26.334 --> 00:04:27.518
Second, air.

82
00:04:27.669 --> 00:04:31.176
Air is a mixture because it contains several different gases.

83
00:04:31.419 --> 00:04:35.669
Third, sodium chloride containing only sodium chloride.

84
00:04:36.114 --> 00:04:37.554
This is a pure compound.

85
00:04:37.797 --> 00:04:39.423
Fourth, seawater.

86
00:04:39.701 --> 00:04:43.765
This is a mixture because it contains water and dissolved substances.

87
00:04:44.287 --> 00:04:45.681
Finally, brass.

88
00:04:46.017 --> 00:04:49.860
Brass is a mixture, more specifically an alloy, mainly containing

89
00:04:49.930 --> 00:04:50.940
copper and zinc.

90
00:04:51.276 --> 00:04:55.224
Now, let us practise a G C S E examination question.

91
00:04:55.618 --> 00:04:59.009
A pure substance melts at eighty degrees Celsius.

92
00:04:59.218 --> 00:05:02.933
A student's sample melts between seventy-four and seventy-nine

93
00:05:02.968 --> 00:05:04.024
degrees Celsius.

94
00:05:04.268 --> 00:05:06.683
Explain what this suggests about the sample.

95
00:05:07.136 --> 00:05:09.121
This question is worth three marks.

96
00:05:09.330 --> 00:05:12.952
A model answer would be: The sample is likely to contain

97
00:05:13.033 --> 00:05:17.108
impurities because it melts over a range of temperatures rather

98
00:05:17.167 --> 00:05:18.885
than at a sharp melting point.

99
00:05:19.338 --> 00:05:22.762
The melting range is also below the melting point of the pure

100
00:05:22.809 --> 00:05:23.563
substance.

101
00:05:23.900 --> 00:05:27.522
Impurities commonly lower and broaden the melting range of a

102
00:05:27.580 --> 00:05:28.079
solid.

103
00:05:28.532 --> 00:05:32.178
Notice how the answer uses the data provided and links

104
00:05:32.236 --> 00:05:34.302
it directly to scientific knowledge.

105
00:05:34.511 --> 00:05:37.008
That is an important examination technique.

106
00:05:37.182 --> 00:05:40.932
Before we finish, remember that pure substances contain only

107
00:05:41.013 --> 00:05:44.798
one element or compound, whereas mixtures contain

108
00:05:44.856 --> 00:05:48.432
two or more substances that are not chemically bonded together.

109
00:05:48.641 --> 00:05:52.391
Pure substances have characteristic melting and boiling points at

110
00:05:52.438 --> 00:05:56.083
a given pressure, while mixtures often melt or boil over

111
00:05:56.164 --> 00:05:56.791
a range.

112
00:05:57.128 --> 00:06:00.774
If you found this explanation helpful, please subscribe to J

113
00:06:00.832 --> 00:06:04.582
D Science and visit j d science dot co dot u

114
00:06:04.651 --> 00:06:08.273
k for more G C S E Chemistry lessons,

115
00:06:08.482 --> 00:06:11.060
worked examples and examination practice.

116
00:06:11.269 --> 00:06:13.939
Thank you for watching, and I will see you in the next

117
00:06:14.009 --> 00:06:14.461
lesson.
